{
  "assertion": "result.length == row.length && Arrays.stream(result).sum() == Arrays.stream(old_row).sum() && Subject.compacted(result) && Subject.validTiles(result) && (Subject.nonzero(result) < Subject.nonzero(old_row) || !Subject.hasMergeablePair(old_row)) && Subject.nonzero(result) >= (Subject.nonzero(old_row) + 1) / 2",
  "explanation": "The strengthened assertion adds two conditions: (1) `Subject.validTiles(result)` ensures the output contains only zeros or powers of two, which holds for the original and all mutants but is a necessary safety check. (2) `Subject.nonzero(result) >= (Subject.nonzero(old_row) + 1) / 2` enforces a lower bound on the number of non-zero tiles after the move. Since each merge reduces the non-zero count by one and a tile can merge at most once per move, the maximum number of merges is floor(nz_old/2), so the minimum possible non-zero count is ceil(nz_old/2). This catches the chain-merge and two-pass mutants (e.g., on [2,2,2,2] they produce 1 non-zero tile, below the minimum of 2).\n\nHowever, the remaining mutants (right-to-left, first-pair-only, sort ascending/descending, slideRight reverse, reverse non-zero) cannot be distinguished using only the four provided helpers because they preserve length, sum, compactedness, validTiles, non-zero count, and the hasMergeablePair boolean on both input and output. To catch them, a new helper is needed that captures the order of non-zero tiles, e.g., `Subject.nonZeroSequence(int[] row)` returning an `int[]` of the non-zero tiles in order, or `Subject.mergedLeftToRight(int[] row)` returning the correct slideLeft result. With such a helper, the assertion could compare the non-zero sequence of the result against the expected left-to-right merge of the input's non-zero sequence."
